title: Tending pays by the errors it prevents more than by the tokens it saves, except in Top's own graph, so it should be gated by what has accrued, not run after every mission.
pid: 76ed6296-ebdb-4e34-84eb-249b238c5fc4
body: |2-
  Inferred, from Scarlett's figures and the sleep-time paper. Work done
  on a graph between missions pays each time the graph is read, and a
  mind reads its whole home note on every waking. A specialist wakes
  once per mission, so cutting what it reads saves cents a mission;
  a tending by Top costs about one wake of Top ($1.60 of Fable), and
  one wrong building mission $11 to $17. So a specialist's graph is
  worth tending for the errors it prevents. Top's graph is the
  exception: on 2026-11-12 and 13 Top spent about $20 a day of Fable
  reading itself in on waking ($32.66 in 38 hours), so a quarter less
  to read there saves about $5 a day, three tendings' worth.
  On when: consolidation forced every round did worst, and its authors
  advise it "selective, batched, and gated" (read); Generative Agents
  reflected when enough had happened, not by the clock (read); Claude
  Code waits for 24 hours and five sessions (summary); a fixed schedule
  fires with nothing to do (Letta, summary). So:
  - After each mission, what is cheap and safe: delete mail once
    absorbed, put a finished plan behind a link that says when to open it.
  - Raise a general lesson above its cases only when three agree, and
    keep in it the conditions under which it holds; with one or two,
    mark it a thing to look for, as Libby's note on plans does.
  - Check claims against their sources and across graphs when a graph's
    waking read has grown or several missions have passed.
  - Schedule no dreaming yet: most graphs have had one to seven missions.
